{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "210",
    "case_summary": "A 34‐year‐old woman presented with pain and slight mobility of the mandibular right second premolar and first molar. Her medical history was unremarkable. On examination, percussion of both teeth was tender, and slight mobility was noted, but the overlying mucosa and skin were normal with no erythema, ulceration, or warmth. Intraoral palpation revealed bony‐hard consistency of the buccal and lingual cortices, and the mucosal integrity was intact.\n\nAn initial orthopantomogram showed a unilocular radiolucent lesion engulfing the apices of the right mandibular second premolar and a previously endodontically treated first molar, extending to the superior border of the inferior alveolar nerve canal; its irregular shape was atypical for routine periapical cysts or granulomas. Cone beam CT further demonstrated buccolingual expansion with almost complete obliteration of the lingual cortex, measuring approximately 23.4 × 14.2 × 6.6 mm.\n\nBased on the radiographic findings, the preliminary differential diagnosis included odontogenic keratocyst, radicular cyst, and unicystic ameloblastoma.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Periapical actinomycosis\nB. Odontogenic keratocyst was considered because of the lesion’s unilocular radiolucency and irregular margins on imaging\nC. Radicular cyst was considered given the lesion’s periapical location associated with a nonvital or endodontically\nD. Unicystic ameloblastoma was considered due to the expansile radiolucent appearance in the mandible, but it was excluded\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
